This row is continuation & elaboration of Row 4;

I) Regarding Istanbul CCC;

Information conferences were held as part of the mission. During the preparation of the İstanbul Climate City Contract, over 200 city stakeholders, including IMM departments, academic institutions, NGOs, and district municipalities, participated in the evaluation of action and investment plans.

Consultants from the EU Commission, NZC project managers and IMM internal department managers discussed related to possible strategic plans.

II) Regarding Build4GreenIST Project Raising-Awareness Activities & Educations;

In cooperation with IMM and ÇEDBİK; Bahçelievler, Üsküdar, Ümraniye, Sultangazi, and Kağıthane Institute İstanbul İSMEK Trainees were provided with trainings on 'Green Buildings, Cities and Energy Efficiency' and a total of 255 citizens were reached. 

This training series aimed to raise awareness of citizens on green buildings, energy efficiency and sustainable cities. Participants were informed about saving energy in their daily lives, adopting environmentally friendly habits and using resources more efficiently. The awareness raised by the training will make it easier for individuals to support green transformation in their own living spaces and for this transformation to become a social movement. 

Beyond the training programs, capacity-building workshops were conducted, bringing together key stakeholders (district municipality staff and academic members) and reaching out a total of 68 participants.

A total of 165 participants were trained on the themes of 'Green Building and Renewable Energy', 'Green Cities and Participatory Transition' and 'Green Finance' with the participation of practitioners from NGOs, district municipalities and relevant departments of IMM. The trainings demonstrated how cities can achieve sustainable, energy-efficient, and environmentally friendly transformation by leveraging academic literature and proven best practices, thereby supporting their transition toward green and carbon-neutral development. 

III) Regarding Build4GreenIST Project Peer to Peer Learning Process;

To foster knowledge exchange and promote the replication of good practices, the project organized B40 city visits to Sarajevo, Zagreb, İzmir, and Muğla, enabling direct experience sharing and collaboration among partner cities. These cities were selected through a structured internal evaluation that emphasized diversity in governance models, geographic contexts, and urban transformation challenges.

IV) Regarding Twinning Learning Programme;

Additionally, through the Twinning Learning Programme, Istanbul has been paired with Greece, Mytilene (Lesvos), enabling targeted peer-to-peer learning and exchange of best practices. Lessons from these visits and partnerships have directly informed the strategic replication framework developed within the project.

O Plano Intermunicipal de Adaptação às Alterações Climáticas do Alentejo Central constitui um instrumento fundamental para promover o planeamento adaptativo ao nível local e sub-regional - a realizar pelos 14 municípios que integram a Comunidade Intermunicipal do Alentejo Central (CIMAC) - através da sistematização e da análise prospetiva do conhecimento climático, da definição de abordagens de adaptação, da sinalização das formas de integração no planeamento local, da capacitação dos técnicos municipais e da sensibilização e mobilização da comunidade em geral. Este Plano pretende dotar o território de Alentejo Central de uma estratégia intermunicipal de adaptação às alterações climáticas, criando condições favoráveis para a dinamização da adaptação à escala local e gerando sinergias entre os vários municípios da sub-região.
Como objetivos específicos pretende:
i) aumentar o conhecimento sobre as alterações climáticas na sub-região e promover inovação na adaptação;
ii) identificar os impactos climáticos, analisar a capacidade adaptativa sub-regional e avaliar as vulnerabilidades atuais e futuras do Alentejo Central;
iii) definir o programa de opções e medidas de adaptação a desenvolver pela CIMAC e pelos municípios de Alentejo Central, com a identificação das potenciais fontes de financiamento;
iv) criar condições técnicas para a integração da adaptação no ordenamento do território e na gestão dos recursos hídricos ao nível intermunicipal e municipal;
v) capacitar técnicos dos municípios de Alentejo Central para a elaboração de estratégias municipais de adaptação, dotando-os das ferramentas metodológicas e dos conhecimentos técnicos e científicos necessários para o desenvolvimento e implementação desses instrumentos;
vi) dotar a CIMAC de um plano de comunicação e divulgação do Plano, que contribua para sensibilizar os atores sub-regionais para as vulnerabilidades e impactes das alterações climáticas e para a necessidade de adotar medidas adaptativas.
A elaboração do Plano está alinhada com a Estratégia Nacional de Adaptação às Alterações Climáticas 2020, procurando estabelecer um caminho adaptativo para todas as áreas temáticas e todos os setores estratégicos pertinentes, nomeadamente: agricultura, florestas, biodiversidade, economia (indústria, turismo e serviços), energia, saúde humana, segurança de pessoas e bens, e transportes e comunicações.

This is a community-based project funded by the National Lottery 'Climate Action Fund'. It seeks to inspire positive action to mitigate the effects of climate change. The project area follows the river valley downstream from Barcombe, through Lewes and the villages of the lower Ouse Valley, to the large coastal communities of Seaford, Peacehaven and Newhaven.
The project is being led by the South Downs Trust along with a number of local partners. These include the South Downs NPA, Lewes District Council, Railway Land Wildlife Trust, Transition Town Lewes, Seaford Community Partnership, Community Energy South, OVESCO, 3VA and Sussex Community Development Association.
The partnership secured an initial £150,000 of development funding to work up a larger project, which aims to bring the whole community together to help the environment and create a more sustainable planet for future generations.
The development stage was complete in April 2022. This involved working directly with local communities to help refine and cost their plans in response to the climate emergency. It is one of 21 projects being taken forward nationally under the Lotteries Climate Action fund.
Project Outline:
The current funding will help the partnership test and develop ways to increase long-term resilience to climate change. The delivery phase of the project, which will commence in October 2022, has a total value of £2.5 Million. It will engage with local communities to deliver a series of long-lasting projects that address the climate emergency and help nature flourish. These will include:
•	Creation of more green corridors and connections to the South Downs National Park;
•	Habitat improvements - such as tree planting, wildflower corridors and improving habitat quality at nature reserves and local wildlife sites;
•	Enhance skills and knowledge around climate change action and mitigation, such as community gardens and orchards, training for local groups and schools, climate cafes, sustainability advice and community-based events;
•	Reduced carbon emissions and energy usage, support residents to reduce their energy bills, training up volunteers as 'climate and energy champions', identify new sites for community owned energy schemes and helping to increase access and usage of electric vehicles and active travel.
